  "result": "The premise is not correct: more agents do not always give better results. So neither five nor ten is the right starting number.\n\nThink of a restaurant. A small café with one cook and one server runs well. If you add a separate person for every step (chopping, seasoning, plating, and a manager to relay messages between them), each handoff costs time and invites errors. Every agent is the same: each one re-reads its instructions and context, and each message between agents is paid for in tokens. Your budget is small, so this matters directly.\n\nSplitting pays off in limited cases:\n- The tasks are independent and can run in parallel.\n- One agent's context would become too large or cluttered to work well.\n- The tasks need very different tools or instructions.\n\nYour assistant does three related things: answer from manuals, check the calendar, and book equipment. That is likely a single agent with three tools, and perhaps a fixed workflow for the booking steps.\n\nMy recommendation is to start with one agent. Split only when you can name a specific failure that the split fixes.\n\nQuick check: which of the three reasons above, if any, applies to your manual lookup, and why?",
